#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096353 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "7b49d6ec-658f-5d2a-b043-a0646ad5f904" :question/math-academy-id "e-4046" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{7 x^2 - 2x - 13 }{(x^2+1)(x+1) } = \\dfrac{9x+B}{ x^2 +1} - \\dfrac{2}{x+1} ,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(x^2+1)(x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2}-2x-13 & =(9x+B)(x+1)-2({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-2(0)-13 & =(9\\cdot (0)+B)(0+1)-2((0)^{2}+1) \\\\ -13 & =B-2 \\\\ B & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "f2483ab1-2728-5a0f-b3e2-7388b5051493" :question/math-academy-id "e-504" :question/problem "Express $\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+4)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{C}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since $x^2+4$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $(x^2+4)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)+3 & =(A(1)+B)(1-1)+C({1}^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =5C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)+3 & =(A(0)+B)(0-1)+C({0}^{2}+4) \\\\ 3 & =-B+4C \\\\ 3 & =-B+4(1) \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2)+3 & =(A(2)+B)(2-1)+C({2}^{2}+4) \\\\ 7 & =2A+B+8C \\\\ 7 & =2A+1+8(1) \\\\ 2A & =-2 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1,$ $B=1,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{(x^2+4)(x-1)} = \\dfrac{1-x}{x^2+4} +\\dfrac{1}{x-1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efd40b21-153e-56ff-b343-99c8aac2ae03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301600} {:answer/id #uuid "89024da1-983c-5a3e-9382-e416645cfce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301601} {:answer/id #uuid "a7649319-8d95-5cf4-b3c7-6c62ca0bbfa6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301602} {:answer/id #uuid "54874472-e059-576e-a4f3-011d082adcd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301603} {:answer/id #uuid "8109e3e0-f7d9-51f0-820e-694823ad001c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89024da1-983c-5a3e-9382-e416645cfce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301601} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f38c4c5-e246-5b02-b089-0d899df8b262" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301599}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe474dc6-fd17-55a5-b177-03917c5838c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-102558" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5x+17}{({x}^{2}+5)(x-2)}=\\frac{Ax-1}{{x}^{2}+5}+\\frac{3}{x-2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+5)(x-2)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+17 & =(Ax-1)(x-2)+3({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}-5(1)+17 & =(A(1)-1)(1-2)+3({1}^{2}+5) \\\\ 17 & =(A-1)(-1)+3(6) \\\\ 17 & =-A+1+18 \\\\ -2 & =-A \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b23b03-0494-583b-b395-95cf280a3791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278739} {:answer/id #uuid "5a864707-d5cb-523e-8971-2242d17049cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278740} {:answer/id #uuid "0a4fcc99-0942-551e-8c1b-315971ef5729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278741} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ea9441-7ce8-56c5-a27c-f9a13ba031ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278742} {:answer/id #uuid "9402b7d7-312c-51bc-aced-3b869b69a242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9402b7d7-312c-51bc-aced-3b869b69a242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278743} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a6de8ce-7452-5b79-9a56-e20a20c1d18e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ca29016-576b-5531-978a-0e93b6677e56" :question/math-academy-id "q-102560" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5x+12}{(2{x}^{2}+3)(x-3)}=\\frac{x+B}{2{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{2}{x-3},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(2{x}^{2}+3)(x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+12 & =(x+B)(x-3)+2(2{x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)^{2}-5(0)+12 & =(0+B)(0-3)+2(2(0)^{2}+3) \\\\ 12 & =-3B+6 \\\\ 6 & =-3B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad8c427-9061-5d56-96b7-aaf7ea7df328" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{1}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266451} {:answer/id #uuid "b43fd6df-ac80-58f7-b04d-6a75e53f78af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x-5}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266452} {:answer/id #uuid "9a20ffb8-6d84-58b9-aaad-4aa88f241da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{4}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266453} {:answer/id #uuid "4242797a-4904-585e-80e9-32b186ba1f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266454} {:answer/id #uuid "1cdbff02-3dae-518a-b942-37e882a23c3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-4}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{5}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4242797a-4904-585e-80e9-32b186ba1f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266454} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f2d2ec3-c0fa-51a0-b5be-8d3b2b96ece2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10213766-0256-5950-8424-1b014b86c8e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-102561" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+6$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)$ and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2x-3=(Ax+B)(x+5)+C({x}^{2}+6).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-5)^{2}+2(-5)-3 & =(A(-5)+B)(-5+5)+C((-5)^{2}+6) \\\\ 75-10-3 & =31C \\\\ C & =\\frac{62}{31} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}+2(0)-3 & =(A(0)+B)(0+5)+C((0)^{2}+6) \\\\ -3 & =5B+6C \\\\ -3 & =5B+6(2) \\\\ 5B & =-3-12 \\\\ B & =\\frac{-15}{5} \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A$ we choose $x=1$, and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}+2(1)-3 & =(A(1)+B)(1+5)+C((1)^{2}+6) \\\\ 2 & =6A+6B+7C \\\\ 2 & =6A+6(-3)+7(2) \\\\ 6A & =2+18-14 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1,B=-3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)}=\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66ceff31-4de7-5e73-b8f4-617aa95482fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301607} {:answer/id #uuid "7e17cec5-946b-57b3-93d6-a53a3fbfec68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+4}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301608} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2129c9-cfa0-5852-ad60-250f0f96e996" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}-\\frac{5}{(x-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301609} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cdb026-7732-59a5-8b47-371bd541f5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301610} {:answer/id #uuid "2a79315d-acb8-5a84-bf21-49c8b9b0ab8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+3}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{1}{(x-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66ceff31-4de7-5e73-b8f4-617aa95482fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301607} :answer-field/id #uuid "050ca3e5-da3e-5612-80f8-6609b89f3e54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "521a4105-19cc-5686-bb26-dc951626ae6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102575" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{6{x}^{2}-8x+5}{({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6{x}^{2}-8x+5}{({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)$ and get\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-8x+5=(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4)^{2}-8(4)+5 & =(A(4)+B)(4-4)+C({4}^{2}+4+3) \\\\ 69 & =23C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)^{2}-8(0)+5 & =(A(0)+B)(0-4)+C({0}^{2}+0+3) \\\\ 5 & =-4B+3C \\\\ 5 & =-4B+3(3) \\\\ 4B & =9-5 \\\\ B & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1)^{2}-8(1)+5 & =(A(1)+B)(1-4)+C({1}^{2}+1+3) \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3B+5C \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3(1)+5(3) \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3+15 \\\\ 3A & =-3-3+15 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=1,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{6{x}^{2}-8x+5}{({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)}=\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3f4ae5f-2a6c-567f-b63c-ad4e4959e404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+2}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278746} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb8a3d-56a5-5e10-94a4-7af107198885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{7}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278747} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e61a2e-1e40-5e75-b293-68a9203b6a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x+2}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{3}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278748} {:answer/id #uuid "6518ffc0-cff6-5554-9615-49971311022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278749} {:answer/id #uuid "a082b5ef-4432-5a26-94b1-5ee1f5d438d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+1}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{1}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6518ffc0-cff6-5554-9615-49971311022d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278749} :answer-field/id #uuid "2da4db48-0dde-5e52-941d-16512a829e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae5b3b02-16d6-5d15-8449-764655c69b3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11007" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+11)}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+11$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-22x-59=(Ax+B)(x-5)+C({x}^{2}+11).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2}-22(5)-59 & =(A(5)+B)(5-5)+C({5}^{2}+11) \\\\ 25-110-59 & =36C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{144}{36} \\\\ C & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{2}-22(0)-59 & =(A(0)+B)(0-5)+C({0}^{2}+11) \\\\ -59 & =-5B+11C \\\\ -59 & =-5B+11(-4) \\\\ 5B & =-44+59 \\\\ B & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}-22(1)-59 & =(A(1)+B)(1-5)+C({1}^{2}+11) \\\\ 1-22-59 & =-4A-4B+12C \\\\ -80 & =-4A-4(3)+12(-4) \\\\ 4A & =-12-48+80 \\\\ A & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=5,$ $B=3,$ and $C=-4.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-22x-59}{({x}^{2}+11)(x-5)}=\\frac{5x+3}{({x}^{2}+11)}-\\frac{4}{(x-5)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096869 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "844192ba-2ead-596b-bbe4-2d01723b0ddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2x + 1)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) - (3)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093622} {:answer/id #uuid "a0833c98-62bb-5d09-a351-74b96ea5041b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 3)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) + (2)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093623} {:answer/id #uuid "ee71b3e3-6b7f-528b-b66f-090604499237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x + 1)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) + (2)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093624} {:answer/id #uuid "30b68480-7100-56df-adce-16e6594ed0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 3)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) - (2)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093625} {:answer/id #uuid "67d9c4d7-b30c-5a90-a731-883e5837afcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x + 2)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) + (3)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee71b3e3-6b7f-528b-b66f-090604499237" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3x + 1)/((x^{2} + 2x + 3)) + (2)/((x - 1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093624} :answer-field/id #uuid "593c74ed-18ae-56d1-b3cd-33c9519975b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093621}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ed7374e-1891-5f2a-9591-44dae68b363d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11014" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)$ and get\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}+2x+5=(Ax+B)(x-1)+C({x}^{2}+2x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+2(1)+5 & =(A(1)+B)(1-1)+C((1)^{2}+2(1)+3) \\\\ 5+2+5 & =6C \\\\ C & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({0}^{2})+2(0)+5 & =(A(0)+B)(0-1)+C({0}^{2}+2(0)+3) \\\\ 5 & =-B+3C \\\\ 5 & =-B+3(2) \\\\ B & =6-5 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=2$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2)^{2}+2(2)+5 & =(A(2)+B)(2-1)+C({2}^{2}+2(2)+3) \\\\ 29 & =2A+B+11C \\\\ 29 & =2A+(1)+11(2) \\\\ 2A & =29-23 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=1,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+2x+5}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)(x-1)}=\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+2x+3)}+\\frac{2}{(x-1)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d10c4317-f4b3-5846-8376-c7d382fc0ba3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Rational Functions as a Sum of Partial Fractions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246a9b61-0e8a-5a21-b8d8-06264175159d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x-3}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266479} {:answer/id #uuid "4f394579-1cbf-5a79-aef6-28430d55ca21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x-8}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{5}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266480} {:answer/id #uuid "dab86164-5e86-582f-a790-f4f2d111cc7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{6}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266481} {:answer/id #uuid "ffed33a2-8876-5902-a220-7a3b0930283a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x+5}{({x}^{2}+1)}-\\frac{3}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266482} {:answer/id #uuid "a41682cd-77eb-5199-9a83-a429790912b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x+2}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{1}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "246a9b61-0e8a-5a21-b8d8-06264175159d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x-3}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266479} :answer-field/id #uuid "8615af00-5ca7-54d3-a212-9aa564da9141" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ba96632-d869-56e1-a1ef-bc4e5fea1ab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-11015" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-28x+17}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-28x+17}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-5)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{C}{(x-5)}.\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+1$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+1)(x-5)$ and get\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-28x+17=(Ax+B)(x-5)+C({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(5)^{2}-28(5)+17 & =(A(5)+B)(5-5)+C({5}^{2}+1) \\\\ 175-140+17 & =26C \\\\ 52 & =26C \\\\ C & =\\frac{52}{26} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-28(0)+17 & =(A(0)+B)(0-5)+C({0}^{2}+1) \\\\ 17 & =-5B+C \\\\ 17 & =-5B+2 \\\\ 5B & =2-17 \\\\ B & =-\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)^{2}-28(1)+17 & =(A(1)+B)(1-5)+C({1}^{2}+1) \\\\ -4 & =-4A-4B+2C \\\\ -4 & =-4A-4(-3)+2(2) \\\\ 4A & =12+4+4 \\\\ A & =\\frac{20}{4} \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=5,$ $B=-3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-28x+17}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-5)}=\\frac{5x-3}{({x}^{2}+1)}+\\frac{2}{(x-5)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3690fc1f-4c84-57fa-b606-75788d3e9b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3690fc1f-4c84-57fa-b606-75788d3e9b06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266486} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ca4568-bc12-5c7f-af12-b55a44d28fac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266485} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "416ace5f-a095-5b1d-9f53-7a89a7595257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "416ace5f-a095-5b1d-9f53-7a89a7595257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266488} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b651625-8eef-5707-9a2f-83184ef2d966" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266487} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f03868e-b94d-5f9b-a926-e9c2b6a05ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f03868e-b94d-5f9b-a926-e9c2b6a05ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266490} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e237352-c2c2-53e3-b6f8-254875dcd5e5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3fb4ddf-fa1a-5f35-80c1-327fb7940b68" :question/math-academy-id "q-346957" :question/problem "Express $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\,$ | {{field-1}}$\\,x\\,+$\n{{field-2}} | $+$ | {{field-3}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $8({x}^{2}+4)$ |  | $8(x+2)$ |" :question/worked-solution "Our task is to find $A$, $B$, and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{C}{x+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)$ gives\n\n$$\n5=(Ax+B)(x+2)+C({x}^{2}+4).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =C(4+4) \\\\ 5 & =8C \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(0)+B)(0+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((0)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =2B+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 2B & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ B & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(1)+\\frac{5}{4})(1+2)+\\frac{5}{8}((1)^{2}+4) \\\\ 5 & =3A+\\frac{15}{4}+\\frac{25}{8} \\\\ 3A & =-\\frac{15}{8} \\\\ A & =-\\frac{5}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{8}x+\\frac{5}{4})}{{x}^{2}+4}+\\frac{(\\frac{5}{8})}{x+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\,x+10}{8({x}^{2}+4)}+\\frac{5}{8(x+2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7cb394b-eda3-5f32-bba0-be1563f59bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095582} {:answer/id #uuid "0935b21e-5995-5cc2-8c0f-cc9fc00b797d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095583} {:answer/id #uuid "1d178e35-12ea-57c0-a17e-4214db24c4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095584} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf5c21a-f0e5-5b87-8f1b-c281e0b326d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095585} {:answer/id #uuid "7a45039f-5376-5780-8be8-e1ab59ba621c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7cb394b-eda3-5f32-bba0-be1563f59bab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095582} :answer-field/id #uuid "4096667f-b42b-52a6-b0c5-36a91deea08d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f7cbdf98-063b-559c-9a18-938783c8c54a" :question/math-academy-id "q-49718" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{({x}^{2}+1)(x+2)}=\\frac{Ax+B}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{C}{x+2},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We multiply both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+1)(x+2)$ and get\n\n$$\n5=(Ax+B)(x+2)+C({x}^{2}+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-2$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(-2)+B)(-2+2)+C((-2)^{2}+1) \\\\ 5 & =5C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(0)+B)(0+2)+(1)({0}^{2}+1) \\\\ 5 & =2B+1 \\\\ 2B & =4 \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =(A(1)+2)(1+2)+1({1}^{2}+1) \\\\ 5 & =(A+2)(3)+2 \\\\ 3A+6 & =3 \\\\ 3A & =-3 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=-1,B=2,$ and $C=1.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\nA+B+C=-1+2+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6490a5-4923-58e8-99c3-d568a033800e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Coefficients in a Partial Fractions Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1feeb5e3-45f2-51a1-a3f6-6472b88c2415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301614} {:answer/id #uuid "47ab84c3-7d44-5521-bbaf-6c183cc4d8ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301615} {:answer/id #uuid "f986a5ea-d071-57cf-a4dd-b02f6efd7ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301616} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cf693c-dc1c-5acc-a3b9-4558015a1ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301617} {:answer/id #uuid "45184ff8-36be-5a4d-b943-eca88602e81d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f986a5ea-d071-57cf-a4dd-b02f6efd7ef4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301616} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dc0a31f-af8c-5a9c-bd61-5eb0c29636f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9e48716-0b82-59b0-97d7-83aff7e756a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-51144" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}+x+3}{({x}^{2}+1)x}=\\frac{Ax+1}{{x}^{2}+1}+\\frac{3}{x},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+1)x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+x+3 & =(Ax+1)x+3({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}+1+3 & =(A(1)+1)(1)+3({1}^{2}+1) \\\\ 9 & =(A+1)+3(2) \\\\ 9 & =A+1+6 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
